---
/**
 * Generic drawn device frame (task 28) — laptop, tablet or phone, with a real
 * screenshot behind the glass.
 *
 * `.astro`, not `.tsx`, deliberately: the frame is a STATIC composition whose
 * only dynamic content is an `astro:assets` `<Image>`, and `<Image>` can only
 * render in `.astro`. The `.tsx` + named-slot dance the case tiles use
 * (`case-card.tsx`) exists because those tiles need React for hover/focus
 * state; here there is no state, no hydration and no island — so the image
 * renders inline instead of being threaded through a slot, and the whole
 * showcase ships zero client JS.
 *
 * The frames are GENERIC hardware: rounded plastic bodies, a thin lit bezel, a
 * single centered camera dot, a laptop deck with a hinge shadow and a thumb
 * scoop. No trade dress of any manufacturer — no notch/island silhouette, no
 * home button, no logos, no branded proportions.
 *
 * Material vocabulary is the jewel case's (global.css `@layer components`):
 * thin gradients and hairline inset edges, dark plastic that does NOT recolor
 * with the theme (a device is a physical object, like the case's edge walls).
 * See `.v8-device-*` in global.css.
 *
 * Task 29 craft pass — four things carry the illusion, all documented at the
 * rules themselves: a many-stop horizontal gradient on the deck's front face
 * (`.v8-device-deck`) so the lip reads as a milled extrusion; a base tapered
 * by clip-path at the top and two-value percentage radii at the foot, lit
 * chamfer-down-to-contact-line with the opener recess cut into its TOP edge
 * (`.v8-device-chamfer`, `.v8-device-scoop*`); a dedicated blurred contact
 * shadow on the floor (`.v8-device-ground`); and ONE hard-stop reflection
 * wedge over the glass (`.v8-device-glare`) in place of the soft sheen this
 * replaced. The last two apply to all three kinds; the first two are the
 * laptop deck.
 *
 * The deck is the base's FRONT LIP viewed from slightly above, so its internal
 * order is fixed and non-negotiable: bright chamfer at the very top, face,
 * darkening into a contact line at the foot, scoop notched down from the top
 * edge. Every one of those is upside down if you build the deck as a top
 * surface instead — see the block comment on `.v8-device-deck`.
 *
 * Geometry is expressed in PERCENTAGES of the frame's own width so a frame is
 * resolution-independent — the composition sets only a width, everything else
 * follows. Two consequences worth knowing when editing the table below:
 *  - `padding: n%` (including `py-`) resolves against the WIDTH, so bezels stay
 *    a constant fraction of the body at any size.
 *  - a single-value percentage `border-radius` would give ELLIPTICAL corners on
 *    a non-square box, so every radius is written as the two-value
 *    `horizontal%/vertical%` form, with the vertical half divided by the box's
 *    aspect ratio — that makes the corners circular again.
 * Every class string is a complete literal (never assembled from parts) so
 * Tailwind's scanner sees it.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro';

export type DeviceKind = 'desktop' | 'tablet' | 'phone';

interface Props {
  kind: DeviceKind;
  src: ImageMetadata;
  alt: string;
  /** Layout box only (width, flex order, overlap margins): set by the
   * composition in `device-showcase.astro`, which owns the arrangement. */
  class?: string;
  /** Resting-pose rotate utilities, also from the composition. */
  pose: string;
  /** Responsive `sizes` hint matching the width the composition gave us. */
  sizes: string;
}

const { kind, src, alt, class: className = '', pose, sizes } = Astro.props;

/* Per-device geometry.
 *
 * THE LAPTOP'S PROPORTIONS ARE THE WHOLE ILLUSION. A first pass drew a deck
 * only 5% wider than the lid and it read, correctly, as "a tablet on a
 * plinth": a big slab balanced on a small base is a stand, not a computer.
 * What says "laptop" before any material detail does is the WIDTH DELTA — on
 * a real machine seen slightly from above the closed deck is far wider than
 * the lid, because you are looking down the deck's foreshortened top surface
 * and along the lid's face. So the desktop kind is laid out as a BLOCK whose
 * width is the DECK's, with the lid centred inside it at 81.5%:
 *
 *   block   width 1, height 0.5866   → aspect ≈ 1.70 : 1
 *   lid     width 0.815 (81.5% of the block), height 0.542 (92.4% of it)
 *   deck    width 1.000, height 0.0446 (7.6% of the block)
 *   ⇒ the deck overhangs the lid by 9.25% of the block on EACH side
 *
 * The deck also carries `-mt-[0.2%]` so it rides ~1px over the lid's bottom
 * edge. Percentage margins resolve against the containing block's WIDTH, so
 * that stays proportional like everything else here; it is small enough not
 * to disturb the block's height (0.5866 → 0.5846) and large enough to absorb
 * the sub-pixel seam that fractional layout heights otherwise leave between
 * two stacked boxes.
 *
 * Laying the block out this way (rather than the lid at 100% and the deck on
 * negative margins) means the width the composition hands us is the width of
 * the whole laptop: nothing overflows its column, and `md:w-[82%]` means what
 * it says. The lid's own aspect follows: 0.542/0.815 → 1000/665.
 *
 * Bezel derivations (LID width = 1):
 *
 * desktop  side bezel 1.175%, top 2.33%, chin 4.65% — the display fills
 *          97.65% × 89.5% of the lid, sitting 3.5% of the lid's HEIGHT below
 *          its top edge (that band is the camera strip). Thin sides, a
 *          slightly deeper chin: that asymmetry is what a laptop lid actually
 *          has, and a uniform bezel is exactly what makes one read as a
 *          tablet. Screen 0.9765 × 0.5952 → 1.64:1.
 *          Body radius 2.2% → 2.2/0.665 = 3.3% vertical, on the TOP corners
 *          only: a lid's bottom edge is the hinge line, which is straight and
 *          square on real hardware. Rounding it, as a first pass did, opened
 *          two crescents of page background between the lid and the deck at
 *          exactly the place the eye checks for contact, and that alone was
 *          enough to make the display look like a separate object resting
 *          behind the base.
 *          Screen radius = 2.2 − 1.175 = 1.025% of the lid = 1.025/0.9765 ≈
 *          1.05% of the screen's width, 1.025/0.5952 ≈ 1.7% of its height.
 * tablet   side bezel 3.6%, top/bottom 5.2%; screen 3/4 portrait →
 *          screen 0.928 × 1.237, body height 1.341 → aspect 1000/1341.
 *          Body radius 5.6% → 4.2% vertical. Screen radius 2.0% of body →
 *          2.2% / 1.6% of the screen box.
 * phone    side bezel 4.2%, top/bottom 6.4%; screen 9/19.5 →
 *          screen 0.916 × 1.985, body height 2.113 → aspect 1000/2113.
 *          Body radius 13% → 6.2% vertical. Screen radius 8.8% of body →
 *          9.6% / 4.4% of the screen box.
 *
 * The lens sits at `top-0` and is pushed down by a percentage of ITS OWN
 * height (percentage translate resolves against the element, so this survives
 * the width/height base mismatch): offset = (topBezel − lens) / 2, expressed
 * in lens-heights. desktop (2.33−0.7)/2 / 0.7 = 116%; tablet (5.2−2.2)/2 / 2.2
 * = 68%; phone (6.4−3.0)/2 / 3.0 = 57%.
 *
 * `lid` is the shell's own layout box inside the frame's block — only the
 * laptop needs one, because only the laptop has a part wider than its screen
 * assembly.
 */
const FRAME = {
  desktop: {
    lid: 'mx-auto w-[81.5%]',
    body: 'aspect-[1000/665] rounded-[2.2%_2.2%_0_0/3.3%_3.3%_0_0] px-[1.175%] pt-[2.33%] pb-[4.65%]',
    screen: 'rounded-[1.05%/1.7%]',
    lens: 'w-[0.7%] translate-y-[116%]',
    ground: 'inset-x-[1%]',
    widths: [800, 1600],
  },
  tablet: {
    lid: 'w-full',
    body: 'aspect-[1000/1341] rounded-[5.6%/4.2%] px-[3.6%] py-[5.2%]',
    screen: 'rounded-[2.2%/1.6%]',
    lens: 'w-[2.2%] translate-y-[68%]',
    ground: 'inset-x-[3%]',
    widths: [320, 640],
  },
  phone: {
    lid: 'w-full',
    body: 'aspect-[1000/2113] rounded-[13%/6.2%] px-[4.2%] py-[6.4%]',
    screen: 'rounded-[9.6%/4.4%]',
    lens: 'w-[3%] translate-y-[57%]',
    ground: 'inset-x-[4%]',
    widths: [200, 400],
  },
} as const;

const f = FRAME[kind];
---

{/* Perspective lives on the frame's own layout box rather than on the row, so
    each device gets its own centered vanishing point: the poses read as three
    product shots placed side by side, not as one wide-angle scene that skews
    whatever sits off-centre. Matches `case-card.tsx`, which perspectives per
    tile for the same reason. */}
<div class={`perspective-distant ${className}`}>
  <div class={`relative origin-bottom ${pose}`}>
    {/* Contact shadow, FIRST in DOM so the hardware sits on it. Anchored to
        the bottom of the whole device (the deck's front edge on a laptop, the
        body's foot on a tablet/phone) and pushed 92% of its own height below
        that line — percentage translate resolves against the element, which
        keeps this width-relative like every other measurement here. Height is
        5% of the device's width via `aspect-*` for the same reason, so the
        shadow scales with the object: a 630px laptop casts ~31px, a 160px
        phone ~8px. (Do NOT size this off `getBoundingClientRect` — the pose
        transform means the reported box is the projected quad's bounding box,
        not the element.) It is a SIBLING of the `.v8-device-cast` element,
        not a child: `drop-shadow` would otherwise re-shadow the shadow, and
        the blend would be isolated by the filter. `-z-10` because a positioned
        element paints ABOVE its in-flow siblings regardless of DOM order —
        without it the shadow's dense top lands on the deck's lit front lip
        and puts it out. The pose transform makes this element a stacking
        context, so the negative index cannot escape behind the page. */}
    <div
      class={`v8-device-ground pointer-events-none absolute -z-10 bottom-0 aspect-[1000/50] translate-y-[92%] ${f.ground}`}
    ></div>
    <div class="v8-device-cast">
      {/* Body. `origin-bottom` above means the tilts pivot on the device's
          feet, so a row of frames stays planted on one floor line. */}
      <div class={`v8-device-shell relative ${f.lid} ${f.body}`}>
        <div class={`relative h-full w-full overflow-hidden bg-[#08080A] ${f.screen}`}>
          {/* The screenshot is the content: full colour, no duotone (unlike the
              cover panel). `object-top` so a tall page crops from the BOTTOM —
              the header/hero of a screenshot is what identifies the product. */}
          <Image
            src={src}
            alt={alt}
            widths={[...f.widths]}
            sizes={sizes}
            loading="lazy"
            class="h-full w-full object-cover object-top"
          />
          {/* One hard-stop reflection wedge in the top-right corner, cut by
              the bezel radius (`rounded-[inherit]`). Screen blend, so it can
              only add light — the UI under it hazes, never disappears. */}
          <div class="v8-device-glare pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 rounded-[inherit]"></div>
          {/* Glass: the recessed hairline around the display. Last, so the
              recess reads over the reflection rather than under it. */}
          <div class="v8-device-glass pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 rounded-[inherit]"></div>
        </div>
        {/* Camera: one small round lens centred in the top bezel. Round and
            centred is the generic form factor — deliberately not a cutout
            silhouette. */}
        <span class={`v8-device-lens absolute top-0 left-1/2 aspect-square -translate-x-1/2 rounded-full ${f.lens}`}></span>
      </div>
      {kind === 'desktop' && (
        /* Deck: the closed base seen almost edge-on, and the widest thing in
           the frame — it spans the WHOLE block while the lid above it spans
           81.5%, so it overhangs by 9.25% a side. That delta, not the
           material, is what makes the silhouette read as a laptop instead of
           a slab on a stand. Height 4.46% of its own width (7.6% of the
           block), tapered by clip-path at the top and by two-value bottom
           radii at the foot. The front face and the opener scoops are
           children so `overflow-hidden` + the deck's radius clip them to the
           wedge. */
        <div class="v8-device-deck relative -mt-[0.2%] aspect-[224/10] overflow-hidden">
          {/* Top chamfer: the lit bevel along the lip's upper edge, carrying
              the hinge shadow in its own top sixth. `top-0` combined with the
              deck's negative margin above is the whole hinge junction — the
              deck rides ~1px over the lid's bottom edge, and that overlap is
              swallowed by this element's dark band, so no page background can
              show between the two at any width or theme. */}
          <div class="v8-device-chamfer absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-[30%]"></div>
          {/* Opener scoop: two halves of 7% of the deck's width, meeting flush
              at the centre, hanging 40% of the deck's height down from the TOP
              edge — a notch milled into the chamfer that you look down into,
              not a dish on the front face. They come after the chamfer so they
              paint over it: the bright bevel is interrupted across the scoop
              and picks back up along the arc of the cut, which is what makes
              the edge read as dipping rather than as being smudged. */}
          <span
            class="v8-device-scoop v8-device-scoop-l absolute top-0 right-1/2 h-[40%] w-[7%]"
          ></span>
          <span
            class="v8-device-scoop v8-device-scoop-r absolute top-0 left-1/2 h-[40%] w-[7%]"
          ></span>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
